.TH DIRNAME 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
dirname \- strip the last component from a path
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dirname
.I path
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dirname
prints
.I path
without its last component, followed by a newline: the name of the
directory that contains
.IR path .
It works on the string alone and does not access the file system, so
.I path
need not exist.
.PP
Both slash and backslash separate components, and a run of separators
counts as one.
Trailing separators are ignored, and then the last component and the
separators before it are removed.
Other separators are kept as they are, so
.B a//b/c/
gives
.BR a//b .
No component is special, so
.B a/..\&
gives
.BR a .
If nothing remains, the result is
.B .\&
for a relative path, including an empty one, and a single separator for
an absolute path, so
.B /usr
gives
.BR / .
.PP
A drive, an ASCII letter and a colon at the start of
.IR path ,
is kept, and the rest of
.I path
is handled as above.
So
.B C:/foo
gives
.BR C:/ ,
and the drive-relative
.B C:foo
gives
.BR C:. ,
which names the current directory of drive C.
.PP
A path that begins with two separators, of either kind, is a UNC path.
Its first two components after them, normally a host and a share, are a
root that is kept like a drive, and the result is never shorter than
that root and a separator.
So
.B //host/share/dir
and
.B //host/share
both give
.BR //host/share/ .
Device paths are UNC paths too:
.B \e\e?\eC:\edir
gives
.BR \e\e?\eC:\e .
.PP
A separator that
.B dirname
adds after a root is a copy of one in
.IR path :
the one that follows the drive or, for any other absolute path, the
first character.
So
.B C:\eusr
gives
.BR C:\e ,
and
.B //host/share\edir
gives
.BR //host/share/ .
.SH OPTIONS
.B \-\-help
is the only option, so any other argument that begins with a dash,
such as
.B \-h
or
.BR \-\-version ,
is a
.IR path .
A first argument of
.B \-\-
is skipped, which matters only for a
.I path
of
.B \-\-
or
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument, and any further arguments
are then ignored.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
the C runtime expands the wildcards
.B *
and
.B ?\&
in the last component of each argument when busybox starts, even in
quoted arguments, as needed for commands typed in
.BR cmd.exe .
A pattern that matches nothing is left as it is.
When the variable is unset, busybox sets it to
.BR 0 ,
so that busybox programs it starts do not expand their arguments again.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox program, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
and
.B LC_ALL
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid command line: no
.IR path ,
or more than one, with a usage message on standard error.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe whose reader has exited, and the result is
4096 bytes or longer.
No message is printed.
.TP
.B 255
Standard output could not be written, as when it is closed or is a pipe
whose reader has exited, and the result is shorter than 4096 bytes.
No message is printed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the directory of a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ dirname C:/w64devkit/bin/gcc.exe
C:/w64devkit/bin
.EE
.RE
.PP
Strip a Windows path, quoted to protect the backslashes:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ dirname \(aqC:\eUsers\eme\eDocuments\e\(aq
C:\eUsers\eme
.EE
.RE
.PP
A name with no directory part is in the current directory:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ dirname main.c
\&.
.EE
.RE
.PP
The directory of a file in a root is the root:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ dirname C:/pagefile.sys
C:/
$ dirname //server/share/dir
//server/share/
.EE
.RE
.PP
Create the directory of an output file before writing it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ out=build/x64/app.exe
$ mkdir \-p "$(dirname "$out")"
$ cc \-o "$out" main.c
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from Unix
.BR dirname :
.IP \(bu 2
Backslashes are separators, and drives and UNC paths have roots.
.IP \(bu 2
Leading double separators begin a UNC path, so
.B dirname //usr
prints
.BR //usr/ ,
and
.B dirname //
prints
.BR /// .
.IP \(bu 2
It takes exactly one
.IR path ,
as POSIX specifies, not several as GNU
.B dirname
does.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.BR \-z ,
and no long option other than
.BR \-\-help .
.IP \(bu 2
A write error gives no message.
When the result is 4096 bytes or longer, a write error other than to a
pipe whose reader has exited goes unreported, with status 0.
.PP
The wildcard expansion described under
.B BB_GLOBBING
can turn one argument into several.
Started from
.BR cmd.exe ,
.B dirname *.c
fails with a usage message when more than one file matches.
.PP
On Windows 10 version 1903 and later,
.I path
may contain any Unicode characters.
On older versions it is limited to the ANSI code page, and in a
double-byte code page, such as Shift JIS, the second byte of a character
is never taken for a separator.
.PP
The output line ends with LF, not CRLF.
.PP
The kit's
.B sh
runs
.B dirname
inside the shell process, without starting a new one, except where the
shell needs a separate process anyway, as for a command substitution or
a pipeline.
Exit status 141 then ends the shell too.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR basename (1),
.BR busybox (1),
.BR realpath (1),
.BR sh (1)
